A||||6||GT Bastard||Valve adjustment question!||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||06-01-2000||12:56 PM||||For some reason, I'm having trouble distinguishing between TDC and BDC.  This is making the Chiltons method fuzzy...  Is it TDC when the compression isn't felt in the spark plug hole and the balancer notch aligns with "0" on the timing marks, or is it when the compression is first felt?  A mechanic here said you can just forget about TDC and do them individually.  He said to just wait till the pushrod is at the top-most point and then tighten them.  WTF?  Then I called my dad and he gave me ANOTHER procedure.  Can somebody shed some light? Thanks fellas...<P>-Bastard||||reg||4
Z||000001||lennyb4||06-01-2000||01:13 PM||||Top dead center (TDC) is when there is compression in the cylinder. The easiest way i have found to achieve this (i know there will be those of you who have a proble, w/this method)is to take a pencil and hold it in the cylinder in question (through spark plug hole). You then slowly turn the engine until you can feel the cylinder touching the pencil. Then turn by hand until you reach TDC.<P>Just my .02*||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||06-01-2000||01:27 PM||||all i can say is DON'T DO IT WRONG!!! i tried the method in chilton's and i bent 6 of the 12 pushrods. expensive mistake. here's what i did.(alot of people might not like this either.) leave the valve covers off and start the engine up. have all rockers loose enough to chatter, but tight enough not to fall off the pushrod. then tighten all rockers one at a time until you hear no chattering. after that, go 1 1/2 more turns on each rocker and you're done. the hardest part is reinstalling the valve covers. maybe try that method. it worked great for me!!||||reg||4
Z||000003||lennyb4||06-01-2000||01:33 PM||||Ive done fine adjustments using 80's method. BUT do yourself a favor. Go to your nearest auto parts store and get oil deflectors (very messy otherwise). They just snap on, are cheap. and still leave you enough clearance to easily tighten everything to specs.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||06-01-2000||01:49 PM||||But what is the easiest way to do this before putting the valve covers on?  Can I just tighten each valve at it's top-most point?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||06-01-2000||02:42 PM||||Lenny's method is OK but you need to know if it is the compression cycle. When you see the pencil going up stuck your finger and feel the preasure. If so, piston is in compression and you keep rotating balancer until timing mark aligns with zero mark. If you don't feel preasure do one more turn and on that one you will feel it. I re-installed my rockers-prods las January and used the factory service manual procedure were you adjust 6 prods on #1 at TDC and then the other 6 at #4 at TDC. But you need to be sure which ones are exactly because the 6 to adjust is a mixture of intake and exaust valves at different pistons. I did not have any problems with that procedure. Just need to do it with caution not to mix things up. The other one with the engine running sounds to messy for me and dangerous, but to each his own...||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||06-01-2000||08:10 PM||||Thanks for the info guys... I ended up going with the Chiltons manual, only I didn't tighten them 1 & 1/2 turn since everyone seems to think that's nuts.  I did a 1/4 turn instead.  It actually wasn't as bad as i had thought.  I just took my sweet-a$$ed time and made sure it was precise.  I'm getting no lash, so I'm happy.||||reg||1
